config AXS15231B
	bool "AXS15231B QSPI LCD display controller driver"
	depends on SPI
	depends on SPI_EXTENDED_MODES
	help
	  Enable driver for the AXS15231B QSPI LCD controller (e.g. the 3.5"
	  320x480 panel on the Guition JC3248W535 / cobantab1a module). The panel
	  is driven over QSPI: the 32-bit command opcode and parameters are sent in
	  1-wire mode and the pixel data in 4-wire (QIO) mode. SPI_EXTENDED_MODES is
	  required for the 4-wire line mode.

	  The pinned Zephyr must let SPI_LINES_QUAD reach the controller: Zephyr
	  before v4.5.0 truncates the operation flags and quietly sends every
	  "quad" transfer on one line. esp-port/apply-zephyr-patches.sh carries the
	  upstream fix for the workspace builds.

config AXS15231B_COLOR_TEST
	bool "Paint color test bands on the panel at startup"
	depends on AXS15231B
	help
	  After the controller is initialized, draw four horizontal bands (red,
	  green, blue, white) across the panel before the status screen is created.
	  For display bring-up only: it answers whether pixel writes reach the panel
	  GRAM and land at the addressed rows, without depending on LVGL. Say N for
	  normal builds.

config AXS15231B_PIXELS_1WIRE
	bool "Send pixel data on a single data line (not QIO)"
	depends on AXS15231B
	help
	  For display bring-up only. Send the pixel phase of every frame on D0
	  with the 0x02 (write) opcode instead of on four lines with 0x32 (write
	  color). The transfer sequence is otherwise byte for byte what the quad
	  path sends, which is what makes it the control experiment: if an image
	  appears this way, the framing, the window addressing and the panel
	  state are all right and only the four-line data phase is broken.

	  Costs a factor of four in pixel bandwidth. Say N for normal builds.

config AXS15231B_WIRE_PROBE
	bool "Log the SPI2 line mode and the GPIO matrix routing at startup"
	depends on AXS15231B
	depends on SOC_ESP32S3
	help
	  For display bring-up only. After the controller is initialized, log the
	  GPIO matrix output routing of the six pads, then send one 1-wire and one
	  quad frame of a known size and log how long each took and what the SPI2
	  line-mode, duplex and clock registers hold afterwards. That is the only
	  way on this SoC to tell four data lines at 1 MHz apart from one data
	  line at 4 MHz, or a routed pad from an idle one. Say N for normal
	  builds.

config AXS15231B_PAD_PROBE
	bool "Sample the six panel pads while a slow pixel frame runs"
	depends on AXS15231B
	depends on SOC_ESP32S3
	help
	  For display bring-up only. Runs one color frame at 8 kHz and samples
	  CS, SCLK and the four data pads every 10 us from a kernel timer, then
	  logs the six captured levels as waveforms. The payload is built from
	  four distinguishable byte lanes, so the log shows which byte reached
	  which line and on which clock. Needs a fine system tick
	  (CONFIG_SYS_CLOCK_TICKS_PER_SEC of at least 100000) for the 10 us
	  sampling to be real. Say N for normal builds.

config AXS15231B_I2C_PROBE
	bool "Ask the controller's touch block over I2C whether the chip is alive"
	depends on AXS15231B
	depends on I2C
	help
	  For display bring-up only. After the controller is initialized, scan the
	  I2C bus for anything that ACKs and then send the reference driver's
	  touch-read request to 0x3B, logging the reply. That traffic does not go
	  through the display bus at all, so it is the one measurement here that
	  cannot be confounded by the QSPI setup: an ACK at 0x3B means the chip has
	  power and is running its own firmware, and touch data means it has come
	  out of sleep. Say N for normal builds.

config AXS15231B_CS_PROBE
	bool "Verify that the CS pad follows the GPIO that is supposed to drive it"
	depends on AXS15231B
	help
	  For display bring-up only. Drive the chip-select pin idle and then
	  asserted, reading the level back from the pad's input register each time
	  (GPIO.in, not the output latch). CS here is GPIO45, which on the ESP32-S3
	  doubles as VDD_SPI and is bit-banged by this driver rather than driven by
	  the SPI block; if the pad does not actually follow, the panel has been
	  held with CS asserted since boot, and a QSPI slave only resynchronises its
	  bit count on a rising CS edge - which would explain bytes that are perfect
	  on the controller side and unintelligible on the panel. Say N for normal
	  builds.

config AXS15231B_GPIO_QSPI_PROBE
	bool "Bit-bang the QSPI frames from GPIO instead of using the SPI block"
	depends on AXS15231B
	depends on SOC_ESP32S3
	help
	  For display bring-up only. Takes the six panel pads away from SPI2 and
	  clock out the frames by hand - one bit per GPIO write pair - first
	  driving every pad both ways and reading it back from the pad itself, and
	  sampling SCLK from the pad while the hand-built clock runs, so that what
	  follows can be trusted. Then sends the whole conversation (SWRESET,
	  SLPOUT, COLMOD, window, a band of pixels, DISPLAY_ON) under four
	  framings, each painting a band of its own color and its own rows, so one
	  look at the glass says which framing the panel understood: 1-wire pixels,
	  4-wire pixels, the clock idling high or low, a slow clock or the fastest
	  the bit-bang can manage. Say N for normal builds.

config AXS15231B_COMMAND_PROBE
	bool "Probe the command path with commands that are visible on the glass"
	depends on AXS15231B
	help
	  For display bring-up only. A few seconds after init, send six commands
	  whose effect a person can see - DISPLAY_OFF/ON, MADCTL with the scan axes
	  swapped and restored (rotates whatever is on the panel, no pixel writes
	  needed), DISPLAY_INVERSION_ON/OFF - twice through, logging each one. This
	  is the only way to tell whether the panel understands our commands at all:
	  if nothing on the glass ever changes, no command has been understood.
	  Say N for normal builds.

config AXS15231B_QSPI_TOUCH_PROBE
	bool "Poll the controller's touch block over QSPI and log the reply"
	depends on AXS15231B
	help
	  For display bring-up only. Every 500 ms, send a touch-read request over
	  the display QSPI bus and log the reply whenever it changes. Kept as a
	  negative-result instrument: the controller's touch block is on I2C, not
	  here (the reference driver creates it with esp_lcd_new_panel_io_i2c() at
	  0x3B), so the silence on this bus is the expected answer and says nothing
	  about the panel. CONFIG_AXS15231B_I2C_PROBE asks the same question on the
	  bus where the block actually lives. Say N for normal builds.
